::ENG2_U01_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The art teacher placed labeled bins near each table so students could return markers, scissors, and glue without crossing the room. The new system helped cleanup finish faster. Question: Which evidence best supports the paraphrase "The labeled bins made cleanup more efficient"?{
~A. The room had tables.#Incorrect. This detail is true but does not prove that cleanup became more efficient.
=B. The art teacher placed labeled bins near each table.#Correct. This detail directly explains why students could clean up faster.
~C. Students used markers.#Incorrect. This names one supply, but it does not explain how the bins helped cleanup.
~D. Glue was one supply.#Incorrect. This detail is too small and does not support the paraphrase.
}

::ENG2_U01_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The soccer coach moved practice to the shaded field during the hottest week of the season. Players were able to complete drills with fewer heat breaks. Question: Which paraphrase is best supported by the passage?{
~A. The players stopped practicing because the field was too shaded.#Incorrect. The passage says players completed drills, not that practice stopped.
~B. The coach canceled every drill during the hottest week.#Incorrect. The passage says players completed drills.
~C. The players asked to remove shade from the field.#Incorrect. The passage does not say players wanted less shade.
=D. The coach changed the practice location to help players work safely during hot weather.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and is supported by the shaded field and fewer heat breaks.
}

::ENG2_U01_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The club posted meeting reminders on the school website and near the front office. Attendance increased because students had more than one place to find the information. Question: Which evidence best supports the paraphrase "The reminders helped more students attend meetings"?{
=A. Attendance increased.#Correct. This directly supports the idea that more students attended.
~B. The club had meetings.#Incorrect. This does not prove that reminders helped attendance.
~C. The front office was near the reminder.#Incorrect. This detail is about location, not the result.
~D. The school had a website.#Incorrect. This is only one place where reminders were posted.
}

::ENG2_U01_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The library created a shelf for books recommended by students. The librarian said the shelf encouraged students to try books their classmates enjoyed. Question: Which explanation best connects the evidence to the paraphrase?{
~A. The shelf was in the library, so the library had books.#Incorrect. This does not explain the purpose of student recommendations.
~B. The librarian said something, so the shelf was not useful.#Incorrect. This contradicts the passage.
=C. Student recommendations helped classmates find books other students liked.#Correct. This connects the evidence to the paraphrase clearly.
~D. The books were on a shelf, so students did not read them.#Incorrect. The passage says the shelf encouraged students to try books.
}

::ENG2_U01_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The science class used a clear jar to model erosion. When water was poured over the soil, students watched small pieces of dirt move away from the surface. Question: Which evidence best supports the paraphrase "The model showed how water can move soil"?{
~A. The class was a science class.#Incorrect. This gives the subject area but does not prove how water moved soil.
=B. Students watched small pieces of dirt move away from the surface.#Correct. This directly shows water moving soil.
~C. The jar was clear.#Incorrect. This explains visibility, not the erosion process.
~D. The class used a jar.#Incorrect. The jar alone does not prove what the model showed.
}

